blurb:
Windows x86-64. rcx = const char *s (Microsoft x64 calling convention). Reverse this function to equivalent C.

func:
    mov   rax, rcx
scan:
    cmp   byte ptr [rax], 0
    je    done
    inc   rax
    jmp   scan
done:
    sub   rax, rcx
    ret

What does this function compute, and what does it return in rax?

Answer:

size_t my_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p != 0) {
        p++;
    }
    return p - s;
}

rax is initialized to a copy of the string pointer and walks forward one byte at a time (inc rax), comparing each byte against zero until it hits the null terminator (je done). Once the loop exits, sub rax, rcx computes the distance traveled -- the scanning pointer minus the original pointer still held in rcx -- which is exactly the string's length, so this is a straightforward hand-rolled strlen.
